# ============================ Practice 3 (질문·방법) ============================
bi_sp_design_dialysis = make_bank_archetype("bi_sp_design_dialysis", "2.4", "3.C", [
    B("A student fills identical dialysis tubing bags with 5%, 10%, or 20% glucose solution, places each bag in a beaker of distilled water for 30 minutes, and then tests the water in each beaker for glucose with a test strip. Which of the following is the dependent variable in this investigation?",
      ("The glucose reading on the test strip for the water in each beaker", "The dependent variable is the quantity measured in response to the manipulated variable."),
      [("The glucose concentration inside each bag at the start", "The starting concentration is the manipulated (independent) variable."),
       ("The volume of distilled water in each beaker", "The volume of water should be the same for all beakers, so it is a controlled variable."),
       ("The length of time that the bags stay in the beakers", "The time should be the same for all bags, so it is a controlled variable.")]),
    B("A student fills a dialysis tubing bag with 10% glucose solution and places it in a beaker of distilled water for 30 minutes. The student then finds glucose in the water in the beaker and concludes that glucose crossed the membrane. Which additional setup is the best negative control?",
      ("A bag filled with distilled water in a beaker of distilled water, with the water in the beaker tested for glucose", "This setup shows what the test strip reads when no glucose is placed in the bag."),
      [("A bag filled with 10% glucose solution in a beaker of 10% glucose solution", "Glucose is on both sides, so this setup does not show a baseline for the test."),
       ("A bag filled with 20% glucose solution in a beaker of distilled water", "This changes the concentration and is a second treatment, not a control."),
       ("A beaker of distilled water that is tested for glucose after the bag is added", "This is the same measurement as the experimental setup and gives no baseline.")]),
    B("A student fills dialysis tubing bags with 5%, 10%, or 20% glucose solution and places each bag in a beaker of distilled water to test how much glucose crosses the membrane. Which of the following factors must be kept the same for all of the bags?",
      ("The volume of solution in each bag and the time in the beaker", "Controlled variables are held constant so that only the starting concentration differs."),
      [("The glucose concentration inside each bag at the start", "This is the independent variable that the student changes on purpose."),
       ("The amount of glucose found in each beaker at the end", "This is the dependent variable that is measured."),
       ("The reading of the test strip for each beaker", "The test strip reading is the measurement of the dependent variable.")]),
], "identify variables and controls in a dialysis tubing diffusion investigation", "fixed fact bank checked against CED 2.4 and science practice 3", est=65)

bi_sp_predict_agar_cubes = make_bank_archetype("bi_sp_predict_agar_cubes", "2.2", "3.B", [
    B("A student prepares agar cubes with sides of 1 cm, 2 cm, and 3 cm. Each cube contains a pH indicator that turns from pink to colorless when acid reaches it. The student places the cubes in dilute acid for 10 minutes and then measures the percent of the volume of each cube that has turned colorless. Which prediction is best supported by the surface area to volume ratios of the cubes?",
      ("The percent of the volume that turns colorless will be greatest in the 1 cm cube.", "The 1 cm cube has the largest surface area to volume ratio, so acid reaches the largest fraction of its volume."),
      [("The percent of the volume that turns colorless will be greatest in the 3 cm cube.", "The 3 cm cube has the largest surface area but the smallest surface area to volume ratio."),
       ("The percent of the volume that turns colorless will be the same in all three cubes.", "The cubes have different surface area to volume ratios, so the percent will differ."),
       ("The percent of the volume that turns colorless will be least in the 1 cm cube.", "The smallest cube has the largest ratio, so a larger fraction of it is reached by the acid.")]),
    B("A student models cells with agar cubes of three different sizes that contain an indicator, and measures how far a diffusing chemical travels into each cube in 10 minutes. The student wants to state a hypothesis about cell size. Which hypothesis can be tested with these data?",
      ("As the size of a cell increases, the fraction of its volume that is reached by a diffusing substance in a given time decreases.", "The agar cubes measure how much of the volume a diffusing chemical reaches in a set time."),
      [("Larger cells contain more ribosomes per unit of volume than smaller cells.", "The cubes do not model ribosomes or any other cell structures."),
       ("Cells with a larger volume divide more slowly than cells with a smaller volume.", "The investigation does not measure cell division."),
       ("Cells with a smaller volume have more chromosomes than cells with a larger volume.", "Cell size does not determine chromosome number and the cubes cannot test it.")]),
    B("Two spherical bacteria have the same shape. Bacterium X has a diameter of 1 micrometer and bacterium Y has a diameter of 4 micrometers. A student predicts how the two cells compare in the exchange of materials with their surroundings. Which prediction is best supported by the surface area to volume ratio?",
      ("Bacterium X can exchange materials more efficiently per unit of volume than bacterium Y.", "The smaller cell has the larger surface area to volume ratio."),
      [("Bacterium Y can exchange materials more efficiently per unit of volume than bacterium X.", "The larger cell has the smaller surface area to volume ratio, so it exchanges less per unit of volume."),
       ("The two bacteria exchange materials equally efficiently because they have the same shape.", "The same shape does not give the same ratio when the sizes differ."),
       ("Bacterium Y has a larger surface area to volume ratio because its surface area is greater.", "The volume increases faster than the surface area as the diameter increases.")]),
], "make a prediction or hypothesis from the relationship between surface area and volume", "fixed fact bank checked against CED 2.2 (surface area to volume ratio, formulas V = 4/3 pi r^3 and A = 4 pi r^2)", est=70)

bi_sp_error_catalase = make_bank_archetype("bi_sp_error_catalase", "3.2", "3.D", [
    B("A student measures the volume of oxygen released when the enzyme catalase breaks down hydrogen peroxide at 10, 25, 37, and 60 degrees Celsius, with one trial at each temperature. Which change would best reduce the effect of random error on the conclusion about how temperature affects the rate?",
      ("Repeating each trial several times at each temperature and calculating the mean rate", "Repeated trials allow random variation to average out and show the typical rate at each temperature."),
      [("Using a different amount of catalase at each temperature", "This adds a second changing variable and so adds error instead of reducing it."),
       ("Reporting only the trial with the highest rate at each temperature", "Selecting one trial hides the variation and introduces bias."),
       ("Adding more temperatures but running each only once", "More temperatures do not reduce the random error in each measurement.")]),
    B("In a catalase investigation, a student leaves the bottle of hydrogen peroxide uncovered on the bench for a day before using it for all of the trials. Hydrogen peroxide slowly breaks down into water and oxygen gas when it is left standing. Which statement best describes the effect of this error on the results?",
      ("Less substrate is available than assumed, so the volume of oxygen measured is lower in every trial.", "A lower concentration of substrate lowers the rate and the amount of product measured."),
      [("The enzyme has a higher activation energy, so the rate is lower in every trial.", "Enzymes lower activation energy and the standing solution does not change that of the enzyme."),
       ("The temperature of the reaction mixture is raised, so the measured volume of oxygen is greater in every trial.", "Standing on the bench does not raise the temperature of the reaction mixtures."),
       ("The error affects only the trial at the highest temperature and has no effect at the others.", "All of the trials used the same bottle, so every trial is affected.")]),
    B("In a catalase investigation the volume of oxygen collected at 60 degrees Celsius is lower than the volume collected at 37 degrees Celsius. A classmate claims the difference is only due to experimental error. Which additional data would best test this claim?",
      ("Repeated trials at both temperatures to see whether the difference is consistent", "Consistent differences across repeated trials indicate an effect and not random error."),
      [("A single additional trial at a much higher temperature, such as 100 degrees Celsius", "One trial at a new temperature does not test the difference between 37 and 60 degrees."),
       ("One additional trial at 37 degrees Celsius that uses a different enzyme", "Changing the enzyme introduces a new variable."),
       ("A measurement of the room temperature in the laboratory on the day of the trials", "The room temperature does not test whether the difference between the two treatments is reliable.")]),
], "identify sources of error and ways to reduce error in an enzyme investigation", "fixed fact bank checked against CED 3.2 and science practice 3.D", est=70)

bi_sp_design_protein_ph = make_bank_archetype("bi_sp_design_protein_ph", "1.7", "3.C", [
    B("A student adds equal volumes of solutions with pH values of 2, 4, 7, and 10 to samples of egg white at 25 degrees Celsius. After 10 minutes the student measures how cloudy each sample is, because denatured protein makes egg white cloudy. Which of the following is the independent variable?",
      ("The pH of the solution added to each sample", "The independent variable is the factor that the student changes on purpose."),
      [("The cloudiness of each sample after 10 minutes", "Cloudiness is the quantity that is measured, which is the dependent variable."),
       ("The temperature of the samples", "The temperature is the same for all samples, so it is a controlled variable."),
       ("The volume of solution added to each sample", "The volume is the same for all samples, so it is a controlled variable.")]),
    B("A student adds equal volumes of solutions with pH values of 2, 4, 7, and 10 to samples of egg white and measures the cloudiness of each sample after 10 minutes. Which of the following should be done so that differences in cloudiness are due to pH?",
      ("Keep the temperature, the volume of egg white, and the time before measuring the same for all samples", "Holding these factors constant isolates the effect of pH on the protein."),
      [("Use a different volume of egg white for each pH", "Different volumes would add a second changing variable."),
       ("Measure the cloudiness of the pH 2 sample after 10 minutes and the pH 10 sample after 30 minutes", "Different times would add a second changing variable."),
       ("Keep the pH of all the samples the same", "The pH is the variable that is being tested and must differ among the samples.")]),
    B("A student predicts that egg white mixed with a solution of pH 2 will be more cloudy than egg white mixed with a solution of pH 7 after 10 minutes. Which result would be the best evidence that the pH 2 solution denatured the protein?",
      ("The sample at pH 2 is cloudier than the sample at pH 7 under the same conditions.", "Increased cloudiness indicates that the protein has unfolded and aggregated."),
      [("The sample at pH 7 is cloudier than the sample at pH 2 under the same conditions.", "This result would indicate more denaturation at pH 7, which is opposite to the prediction."),
       ("The sample at pH 2 has the same cloudiness as the sample at pH 7.", "Equal cloudiness would indicate no effect of pH."),
       ("The sample at pH 2 is warmer than the sample at pH 7.", "Temperature differences do not measure denaturation of the protein.")]),
], "identify variables and controls in a protein denaturation investigation", "fixed fact bank checked against CED 1.7 and science practice 3", est=65)

bi_sp_design_phenotype_environment = make_bank_archetype("bi_sp_design_phenotype_environment", "5.5", "3.C", [
    B("A researcher grows genetically identical clones of a plant at 15, 20, 25, and 30 degrees Celsius and measures the height of each plant after six weeks. The researcher wants to test whether temperature affects plant height. Why is it important that the plants are genetically identical?",
      ("It ensures that differences in height are due to the temperature and not to differences in genotype.", "Identical genotypes remove genetic variation as an explanation for the differences."),
      [("It ensures that all of the plants will have the same height at every temperature.", "Identical genotypes can still have different phenotypes in different environments."),
       ("It ensures that temperature has no effect on the phenotype.", "The purpose is to test the effect of temperature, which can affect phenotype."),
       ("It increases the number of different alleles in the plants.", "Clones have the same alleles, so this does not increase the number of alleles.")]),
    B("A researcher grows genetically identical clones of a plant at four different temperatures and measures the height of each plant after six weeks. Which of the following should be kept the same for all of the plants?",
      ("The amount of water, the light, and the length of time of growth", "Other environmental factors are held constant so only temperature differs."),
      [("The temperature at which each plant is grown", "Temperature is the independent variable that the researcher changes."),
       ("The height of each plant after six weeks", "Height is the dependent variable that is measured."),
       ("The phenotype of each plant at the end of the study", "The phenotype is the measured outcome and is expected to differ.")]),
    B("Some flowers of the same plant species are blue in acidic soil and pink in basic soil. A student wants to test whether soil pH determines flower color. Which design is the best way to test this?",
      ("Grow genetically identical plants in soils of different pH under otherwise identical conditions and record flower color", "Only the soil pH differs, so any difference in color can be attributed to it."),
      [("Grow genetically different plants in one soil of intermediate pH and record flower color", "Without varying the pH, the effect of pH cannot be tested."),
       ("Grow genetically identical plants in soils of different pH with different amounts of light and record flower color", "Light would be a second variable that changes along with pH."),
       ("Grow plants of different species in soils of different pH and record the height of each plant", "Different species and height do not test the effect of pH on flower color.")]),
], "identify the control of genotype and environment in an investigation of environmental effects on phenotype", "fixed fact bank checked against CED 5.5 and science practice 3", est=65)

bi_sp_hypothesis_flycross = make_bank_archetype("bi_sp_hypothesis_flycross", "5.3", "3.A", [
    B("A student crosses fruit flies that are heterozygous for a gene for wing length (long wings are dominant) with flies that have short wings. The student counts the offspring. Which is the best testable hypothesis for the investigation?",
      ("The offspring will have long wings and short wings in a 1:1 ratio.", "A test cross of a heterozygote and a homozygous recessive is predicted to give a 1:1 ratio."),
      [("The offspring will all have long wings.", "Half of the offspring inherit the recessive allele from the heterozygous parent and have short wings."),
       ("The offspring will have long wings and short wings in a 3:1 ratio.", "A 3:1 ratio is expected from a cross of two heterozygotes."),
       ("The long-winged flies will be better at flying than the short-winged flies.", "Flying ability is not measured by counting the offspring.")]),
    B("A student wants to find out whether two traits in pea plants, seed shape and seed color, are inherited independently. The student crosses plants that are heterozygous for both traits and counts the phenotypes of the offspring. Which of the following is the best testable question?",
      ("Do the phenotypes of the offspring appear in a 9:3:3:1 ratio?", "A 9:3:3:1 ratio is expected for two independently assorting genes in a cross of double heterozygotes."),
      [("Why do pea plants have seeds that are round or wrinkled?", "This question asks for a reason and is not answered by counting offspring phenotypes."),
       ("Which of the two traits is more important to the pea plant?", "Importance to the plant cannot be tested by counting offspring."),
       ("Do the offspring of heterozygous parents have a 1:1 ratio of phenotypes?", "A 1:1 ratio is not expected from a cross of two double heterozygotes.")]),
    B("A student plans to test the hypothesis that the gene for eye color in fruit flies is inherited in a simple Mendelian pattern with red eyes dominant to brown eyes. The student crosses two heterozygous red-eyed flies. Which result would be most consistent with the hypothesis?",
      ("About three-fourths of the offspring have red eyes and one-fourth have brown eyes.", "A cross of two heterozygotes gives a 3:1 ratio of dominant to recessive phenotypes."),
      [("About half of the offspring have red eyes and half have brown eyes.", "A 1:1 ratio is expected from a test cross and not from a cross of two heterozygotes."),
       ("All of the offspring have red eyes.", "About one-fourth of the offspring are expected to be homozygous recessive."),
       ("About one-fourth of the offspring have red eyes and three-fourths have brown eyes.", "This is the reverse of the ratio expected for a dominant red allele.")]),
], "state a testable hypothesis or expected ratio for a genetic cross", "fixed fact bank checked against CED 5.3 and science practice 3", est=65)

bi_sp_design_choice_chamber = make_bank_archetype("bi_sp_design_choice_chamber", "8.1", "3.C", [
    B("A student places 10 woodlice in the center of a choice chamber that has a damp side and a dry side and counts how many woodlice are on each side after 10 minutes. To determine whether the woodlice respond to moisture and not to the position in the chamber, which procedure is best?",
      ("Repeat the experiment with the damp and dry sides switched and compare the results", "Switching the sides separates the effect of moisture from the effect of position in the chamber."),
      [("Repeat the experiment with the chamber in a warmer room", "This changes the temperature and adds a second variable."),
       ("Repeat the experiment with more woodlice on the damp side at the start", "Different starting positions would bias the result and not test the response to moisture."),
       ("Repeat the experiment only if the first result shows more woodlice on the damp side", "Repeating selectively introduces bias.")]),
    B("A student places 10 woodlice in a choice chamber that has a dark side and a light side, with the same temperature and humidity on both sides, and counts the woodlice on each side after 10 minutes. Which of the following is the dependent variable?",
      ("The number of woodlice on each side of the chamber", "The dependent variable is the response that is measured."),
      [("The amount of light on each side of the chamber", "The light is the factor that differs between the sides, which is the independent variable."),
       ("The temperature of the chamber", "The temperature is the same on both sides, so it is a controlled variable."),
       ("The total number of woodlice that are placed in the chamber", "The same number of woodlice is used in each trial, so it is a controlled variable.")]),
    B("A student places 10 woodlice in a choice chamber that has a dark side and a light side and counts the woodlice on each side after 10 minutes. The student concludes that woodlice prefer darkness after one trial. Which change would best make the conclusion more reliable?",
      ("Repeat the trial several times with new groups of woodlice and compare the results", "Repeated trials with new groups show whether the response is consistent."),
      [("Repeat the trial with the same woodlice after they have been in the dark for a day", "This changes the condition of the animals and adds a variable."),
       ("Count the woodlice again after one hour and use only the later count", "A new time is a different measurement and does not address the reliability of the first result."),
       ("Use only the trial in which most woodlice are in the dark", "Selecting trials introduces bias.")]),
], "choose a design or control that isolates a behavioral response in a choice chamber", "fixed fact bank checked against CED 8.1 and science practice 3", est=65)

bi_sp_error_quadrat = make_bank_archetype("bi_sp_error_quadrat", "8.4", "3.D", [
    B("A student estimates the density of dandelions in a large field by counting the dandelions in five square quadrats that the student places in the part of the field that is closest to the path. Which statement best describes a source of error in this method?",
      ("The quadrats are not placed at random, so the estimate may not represent the whole field.", "Random placement of many quadrats is needed for the sample to represent the field."),
      [("The quadrats are square, so the dandelions cannot be counted.", "The shape of the quadrat does not prevent counting."),
       ("The student counts the dandelions in each quadrat, so the data are not quantitative.", "Counts are quantitative data."),
       ("The student uses five quadrats, which is too many to estimate a density.", "More quadrats generally improve the estimate and are not a source of error.")]),
    B("A student estimates the number of clover plants in a large field by counting the plants in 5 quadrats of 1 square meter. Another student counts the clover in 30 quadrats of 1 square meter placed at random. Which statement best compares the two estimates?",
      ("The estimate from 30 quadrats is likely to be closer to the true density because a larger sample reduces the effect of chance variation.", "A larger random sample reduces sampling error."),
      [("The estimate from 5 quadrats is likely to be closer to the true density because fewer quadrats reduce mistakes in counting.", "Fewer quadrats increase sampling error."),
       ("The two estimates are equally reliable because both use quadrats of the same size.", "The number of samples affects reliability even when the quadrat size is the same."),
       ("The estimate from 30 quadrats is less reliable because the quadrats are placed at random.", "Random placement reduces bias and does not make the estimate less reliable.")]),
    B("A student counts the number of snails in quadrats along a line from a pond to a dry field to investigate how the density of snails depends on the distance from the pond. The student counts only in the morning, when snails are most active, on the first day and in the afternoon on the second day. Which statement best describes the effect of this procedure?",
      ("The time of day differs among the counts, which adds a variable that could affect the number of snails seen.", "Activity changes with the time of day, so differences in counts could be caused by when the counts are made."),
      [("The distance from the pond is the same for all counts, which prevents the test of the hypothesis.", "The distance is the independent variable and is expected to differ among the quadrats."),
       ("The use of quadrats makes it impossible to compare the number of snails at different distances.", "Quadrats of the same size allow comparisons."),
       ("Counting on two days always makes the results more accurate than counting on one day.", "Counting on two days with different conditions can add error.")]),
], "identify sources of sampling error and improve a field sampling procedure", "fixed fact bank checked against CED 8.4 and science practice 3.D", est=70)

